---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AnswerBox from '../components/AnswerBox.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../data/business';
const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Financing', path: '/financing/' }];
const quickAnswer = 'Trusted Termite and Pest Solutions offers financing on larger jobs such as termite treatments, on agreed terms. We also take cash, check, card in person or online, and Venmo, CashApp or Zelle. There are no contracts on any service.';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Financing &amp; Payment | Trusted Termite &amp; Pest"
  description="Financing available on larger jobs like termite treatment, on agreed terms. Cash, check, card, Venmo, CashApp and Zelle accepted. No contracts."
  path="/financing/"
  quickAnswer={quickAnswer}
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <section class="band">
    <div class="narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Paying for the work</p>
      <h1>Financing and payment</h1>
      <AnswerBox text={quickAnswer} />
      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Financing</h2>
      <p>Financing is available on larger jobs — termite treatments in particular — {BUSINESS.financing.note}. A termite job is rarely a small number, and spreading it should not be the thing that stops the work getting done. Ask when you call.</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">How you can pay</h2>
      <ul class="tight">{BUSINESS.payments.map(p => <li>{p}</li>)}</ul>
      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Discounts</h2>
      <p>Military discount. That is the one we offer, and we would rather list one real discount than a page of them.</p>
      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">No contracts</h2>
      <p>None. Recurring plans run bi-monthly or quarterly and you can stop whenever you want.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Why there is no price list on this site</h2>
      <p>
        You will not find a table of prices here, and it is worth explaining why rather than letting you
        assume the worst. A published number without a look at the property is one of two things. Either
        it is set high enough to cover the difficult version of the job, in which case the majority of
        customers are being quoted for somebody else's problem, or it is set low enough to win the click,
        in which case it is not the number that appears on the invoice and everybody involved knows it
        before the truck arrives.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second version is the one that has trained people in this region to distrust pest control
        pricing, and with reason. A headline figure for "pest control starting at" is nearly always scoped
        to a small in-town house with no outbuildings, no acreage and no unusual pest, quoted as the first
        visit of a recurring agreement whose subsequent visits carry the actual economics. The number is
        true and it is also not an answer to the question you were asking.
      </p>
      <p>
        What we will do instead is tell you exactly what drives the number, so that when you get a quote —
        from us or from anyone — you can tell whether it makes sense.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">What actually determines the price</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>The size and type of the structure.</strong> Square footage matters less than perimeter
        and construction. A long single-storey ranch house has more linear foundation than a compact
        two-storey of the same floor area, and the perimeter is where most of the work happens. Pier-and-
        beam adds a crawl space. A slab does not.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>How many structures there are.</strong> On acreage this is usually the largest single
        factor and the one most often left out of the initial phone call. A house, a shop, a barn and a
        well house is four buildings, and quoting it as one house helps nobody — least of all you, when
        the visit runs out of time before the outbuildings are done.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What the pest is.</strong> General pest work, scorpion work, rodent work and termite work
        are different jobs with different materials, different equipment and very different labour. They
        are not interchangeable line items with a common rate.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>One-time or recurring.</strong> A single treatment carries the full cost of the visit. A
        recurring schedule spreads the setup across the year and is, for most properties in this region,
        genuinely the cheaper way to stay ahead of the problem — not because we would rather sell it, but
        because West Texas pest pressure is seasonal and relentless rather than occasional.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Where the property is.</strong> Distance from Seminole is a real cost on a one-truck
        operation and it would be dishonest to pretend it is not. It is also not applied as a surprise
        line item at the end; if distance affects your number, it will be part of the quote you agree to
        before anything happens.
      </p>
      <p>
        What does not determine the price: how urgent you sound, whether it is the busy part of the
        season, whether you called a competitor first, or whether the inspection revealed that you did not
        know what you were looking at. Those are all real practices in this trade. None of them happen
        here.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">How quoting works in practice</h2>
      <p>
        Most general pest work can be quoted accurately over the phone, provided you can answer the
        handful of questions above. That is deliberate — driving out to look at a standard three-bedroom
        house in order to quote a standard treatment wastes your afternoon and ours, and the cost of that
        wasted drive ends up in somebody's pricing eventually.
      </p>
      <p>
        Termite work is different and effectively always requires an inspection first. The treatment plan
        depends on the construction type, the soil, the grade, whether there is a slab or a crawl space,
        where the activity is and what the structure is doing at the points where it meets the ground.
        Anyone who quotes a termite job over the phone without seeing it is either guessing high or
        planning to revise the number once they arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Where a job is large enough to warrant it, the quote is written. Texas requires the licence number
        to appear on a written estimate for structural pest control work, which is a useful side effect:
        a written quote is also a licence disclosure, and you should have both before money changes hands.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the inspection changes the scope — and inspections do change scope, because they find things
        the phone call could not — the revised number is discussed before any work is done. There is no
        version of this in which you learn about additional charges from the invoice.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Financing, in more detail</h2>
      <p>
        Financing exists here for one honest reason: termite treatment is expensive relative to what most
        households have sitting in an account on the day they find out they need it, and the cost of
        delay is not zero. Termites do not pause while you save up. A structure with active subterranean
        termites in it is accruing damage on a schedule of its own, and the difference between treating
        this month and treating in eight months can be substantial and invisible until it is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Terms are agreed case by case rather than published as a standard product, because this is a
        small business arrangement between two people and not a lending programme. What that means
        practically: ask, early, and it becomes part of the conversation about the work rather than an
        awkward afterthought once the quote lands. Nobody here is going to be surprised or offended by
        the question. It gets asked constantly.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also means the answer depends on the job. Financing is aimed at larger work — termite in
        particular — rather than at routine service, because spreading a small recurring payment over
        instalments does not help anybody and just creates paperwork.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">The payment methods, and what each one means</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Cash and check.</strong> Both fine, both still common here, and both get a receipt.
        Ask for the receipt if one is not offered — not because of us, but because it is a habit worth
        having with any trade that works on your property.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Card, in person or online.</strong> Taken on site or sent as a link, whichever suits.
        Useful for landlords and property managers who need the transaction to sit on a specific account
        rather than being reimbursed later.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Venmo, CashApp and Zelle.</strong> Convenient, widely used, and worth one word of caution
        that applies to every trade and not only this one: confirm the handle with the person you are
        actually dealing with, on the phone, before sending anything. Impersonation of small local
        contractors on payment apps is a live problem, and the safest habit is never to pay a handle you
        received only in writing.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Billing for landlords, managers and businesses</h2>
      <p>
        If you own rental property, manage property for someone else, or are buying service for a
        business, say so on the first call. It changes the paperwork more than it changes the work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Practical things worth sorting up front: who authorises the job, who receives the invoice, whether
        the tenant is the point of contact for access, and whether you need a certificate of insurance on
        file before a vendor comes on site. Most commercial clients do. Sorting that on the phone takes
        four minutes; sorting it on the day costs a visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        For multi-property owners, service across several addresses can be scheduled together and
        invoiced together, which is usually cheaper and always less administration than treating each one
        as an unrelated call.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Budgeting for pest control across a year</h2>
      <p>
        For most households out here, the useful way to think about this is not as an emergency purchase
        but as a small annual line, because the pressure is seasonal and predictable rather than random.
      </p>
      <p>
        A property that gets ahead of the year — perimeter established before spring, exclusion done
        before autumn, the lot kept down through summer — spends less over twelve months than one that
        reacts to each problem as it arrives, and spends it in smaller pieces at predictable times. The
        expensive version of this trade is the one where every call is urgent, because urgency compresses
        the work into the worst weeks and removes every cheap option from the table.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other cost that is easy to miss is the one that is not on any invoice. A rodent problem left
        for a season is wiring, insulation and stored belongings. A termite problem left for a year is
        structure. Neither of those shows up in a price comparison between two quotes, and both of them
        are usually larger than the difference being compared.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Who pays — owners, renters and the split in between</h2>
      <p>
        This question comes up constantly and the answer is less obvious than either side usually
        assumes, so it is worth setting out plainly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Texas property law places an obligation on residential landlords to repair or remedy conditions
        that materially affect the physical health or safety of an ordinary tenant, and infestations can
        fall inside that depending on the circumstances and on what the lease says. Many leases also
        address pest control directly, assigning it to one party or the other, sometimes splitting it by
        pest type or by how long the tenant has occupied the property.
      </p>
      <p>
        What that means in practice: read your lease before you assume. If you are a tenant with an
        infestation you did not create, the request to your landlord is a legitimate one and should be
        made in writing so there is a record of the date. If you are a landlord, treating promptly is
        usually both the cheaper and the safer course, because a pest problem left in place across a
        tenancy tends to become a structural problem across several.
      </p>
      <p>
        We are not lawyers and nothing here is legal advice — the lease and the statute govern, not our
        opinion. What we can do is be practical about it: we will speak to whoever is authorised to
        approve the work, invoice whoever is paying, and coordinate access with whoever is living there,
        without needing those to be the same person. That is normal for us and it does not slow anything
        down.
      </p>
      <p>
        The one thing that does cause problems is when a tenant books a service that the landlord has not
        approved and expects the landlord to pay afterwards. Sort the authorisation before the visit, not
        after.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">What you are actually paying for</h2>
      <p>
        Pest control looks, from the outside, like somebody spraying for twenty minutes. It is a fair
        impression and it is the reason people find the pricing hard to reconcile. Here is what sits
        behind the number, because a customer who understands the cost structure is much harder to
        overcharge — by anybody.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Licensing and continuing education.</strong> The licence is not a one-off fee. It is
        examined, maintained on a cycle, and requires ongoing continuing education in each held category.
        Four categories means four sets of obligations. This is a real annual cost and it is one of the
        things separating a licensed operator from the man with a sprayer who quoted you half.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Insurance.</strong> General liability coverage carried year-round, whether or not it is
        ever claimed on, at limits that mean something. An uninsured operator is cheaper for exactly as
        long as nothing goes wrong on your property.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Materials.</strong> Professional products are not retail products at a markup. They are
        different formulations, bought in different quantities, applied at label rates that are legally
        binding rather than advisory, and stored and transported under rules. The label is the law in
        pesticide application, and a company applying at the correct rate is using more product than one
        that is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Equipment.</strong> Sprayers, dusters, granular spreaders, bait stations, termite
        equipment, protective gear, and the truck that carries all of it across a route this size.
        Equipment in this trade is consumed rather than owned.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Travel.</strong> The distances out here are the distances. An hour of driving to reach a
        forty-minute job is a real hundred minutes of the working day, and any operator who claims
        distance is free is recovering it somewhere you cannot see.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The time on your property.</strong> Which is the part you actually see, and on a properly
        performed first visit it is considerably longer than twenty minutes, because the inspection is
        the job and the application is the consequence of it.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">How to compare two quotes properly</h2>
      <p>
        Getting a second quote is sensible and we would never discourage it. Comparing them badly is
        worse than not comparing at all, and the usual mistake is comparing the numbers rather than the
        scope behind them.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compare the covered pests.</strong> One quote covering general household pests and one
        covering general household pests plus scorpions are not the same product, and in this region that
        difference matters more than almost any other. Ask each company to name what is in and what is
        out.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compare the structures.</strong> If one quote includes the shop and the other does not,
        the cheaper one is not cheaper. Ask explicitly whether outbuildings are in scope.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compare the interior treatment.</strong> Some quotes are exterior-only by default. That is
        a legitimate approach and often the right one, but it needs to be a decision rather than a
        surprise.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compare what happens at visit two.</strong> A low first visit followed by higher recurring
        visits is the standard structure in this industry. Ask for the recurring number, not the
        introductory one, and multiply it out across a year. That is the number to compare.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compare the guarantee.</strong> Duration, covered scope, remedy, and whether it survives
        cancellation. A meaningful thirty-day guarantee is worth more than a vague open-ended one.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Compare the licences.</strong> Both companies, checked against the Texas Department of
        Agriculture records, including whether the categories cover the work being quoted. Two minutes,
        free, conclusive.
      </p>
      <p>
        If after all that the other quote is genuinely better for your situation, take it. We would rather
        lose a job to a fair comparison than win one because the customer could not see what they were
        buying.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">Warning signs in a pest control quote</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>A price given without a single question about the property.</strong> Nobody can price a
        job they know nothing about. A number offered before the structure, the pest and the location have
        come up is a number that will change.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Pressure to decide today.</strong> "This price is only good if I book you now" is a sales
        technique, not a scheduling constraint. Pest problems that are genuinely urgent are urgent
        tomorrow too.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Refusal to give a licence number.</strong> Already covered above, and it remains the
        single most useful question you can ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>A termite quote given over the phone.</strong> Not possible to do honestly. Treat it as
        a placeholder at best.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>An annual agreement produced at the door.</strong> Read the term, the auto-renewal, the
        cancellation clause and the early-termination fee before signing anything on a doorstep. This is
        where most of the regret in this industry is generated.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Fear as the pitch.</strong> A quote built on what might be inside your walls, unsupported
        by anything found during an inspection, is selling anxiety. Real findings get shown to you.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">If the whole job is not affordable right now</h2>
      <p>
        Say so. It is a much more common conversation than people expect and it has practical answers
        beyond financing.
      </p>
      <p>
        Work can often be staged by priority. If a property has active termites in one area and a general
        pest issue elsewhere, the termite work is the thing accruing damage and the other can wait. If
        several outbuildings need attention, the one with the feed in it is doing more for the population
        than the one with the lawnmower. Sequencing by what is actually driving the problem gets most of
        the benefit for a fraction of the immediate outlay.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some of the highest-value work is also free and yours to do: moving the woodpile off the wall,
        fixing the irrigation head that throws against the foundation, replacing a worn door sweep,
        clearing the storage stacked against the exterior. None of that is a substitute for treatment,
        but it changes the pressure on the structure and it makes whatever treatment you can afford work
        harder and last longer. Ask during the inspection which items on your property would move the
        needle most. You will get a straight answer rather than a shrug toward the full package.
      </p>

      <h2 style="margin-top:2.2rem">The military discount</h2>
      <p>
        One discount, offered to military. It is listed here on its own rather than buried in a page of
        promotional badges, because a single real discount that is always available is worth more than a
        rotating list of offers that require a code, an expiry date and a minimum spend.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no seasonal sale, no first-visit-free loss leader and no "mention this website" gimmick.
        The price is the price, it is explained before the work, and it does not move depending on how
        you found us.
      </p>
  <Cta />
</BaseLayout>
